Falsifies `intrusive_queue::verification::any_push_pop_interleaving_is_first_in_first_out_and_lossless`.

The defect: popping the last node leaves `tail` pointing at it. The line that clears it reads as
tidying, because `head` is what `is_empty` and `pop_front` consult and it is already correct;
nothing goes wrong until the queue is emptied and then pushed to again, which no single-push
test does.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(q.is_empty(), m_head == m_tail)`, on the sequence
push, pop, push. The stale tail takes the `Some` arm of the push, so the new node is linked
behind a node that has already left the queue and `head` stays `None`: the queue reports itself
empty while holding a node, and that node is lost. In the kernel this queue holds runnable
threads.

diff --git a/crates/intrusive_queue/src/lib.rs b/crates/intrusive_queue/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/intrusive_queue/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/intrusive_queue/src/lib.rs
@@ -212,5 +212,2 @@ impl<T: Node> Queue<T> {
             self.head = (*node.as_ptr()).next();
-            if self.head.is_none() {
-                self.tail = None;
-            }
             (*node.as_ptr()).set_next(None);
